# AGENTS.md

## Purpose

This file defines the mandatory rules for AI agents and human contributors working on this project.

Agents must read this file before making any change.

The goal of this project architecture is to be:

- Go-first
- stdlib-first
- SQL-first
- self-hostable
- modular
- explicit
- easy to audit
- easy to run locally
- friendly to AI-assisted development
- ready to evolve from a modular monolith into separated services only when justified

Do not ignore this file.

When in doubt, follow this file over assumptions, framework defaults, or previous habits.

---

# 1. Core Architecture

## 1.1 Architectural Style

This project follows a:

```txt
Single Binary Modular Monolith with Embedded React SPA
```

The default architecture is:

```txt
React SPA / PWA-ready
        ↓
Go BFF / HTTP Server
        ↓
Go application modules
        ↓
{{.Stack.Database.Primary}} + {{.Stack.Database.Cache}} + {{.Stack.Messaging.Provider}} + {{.Stack.Database.ObjectStorage}}
```

The system must start as a modular monolith.

Do not split modules into separate services unless there is a documented architectural reason.

Valid reasons for service extraction include:

- independent scaling requirement
- independent deployment requirement
- clear domain boundary
- operational isolation
- security isolation
- long-running workload isolation
- team ownership separation

Premature microservices are not allowed.

---

## 1.2 Main Runtime Model

The project builds a single Go binary.

The binary exposes multiple commands:

```bash
app serve
app migrate up
{{if .Features.Workers}}app worker outbox
app worker files
app worker notifications
{{end}}```

The same binary may be used by multiple containers with different commands.

Example:

```yaml
services:
  app:
    image: {{.ProjectName}}
    command: ["app", "serve"]
{{if .Features.Workers}}
  worker-outbox:
    image: {{.ProjectName}}
    command: ["app", "worker", "outbox"]

  worker-files:
    image: {{.ProjectName}}
    command: ["app", "worker", "files"]
{{end}}```

The React SPA build is embedded into the Go binary using `go:embed`.

